pr_body:
This changes is about to fix #4301 issue.
I've made class `ScheduledTaskQueue` which implements a priority queue by next run time for bunch of tasks. Interface is pretty simple:
  - `void add(TaskImplementation task, UnixTime firstRunTime = 0)` - add task to the queue. The firstRunTime (unix timestamp in seconds) is optional. If it is zero - the task will be performed ASAP.
task - is `std::function<UnixTime(UnixTime)>` object. It is a task definition. It get real start time as a first argument and should return next time of run. If it returns zero, the task will be never performed again and will be deleted from the queue.
  - `bool isEmpty() const` - method to check if queue is empty.
  - `UnixTime timeToWait() const` - method to know the time to the next task can be performed.
  - `void runOne()` - Run task from the head of the queue and reinsert it into the queue if the return value is not zero. It will be performed regardless of required run time. Be sure that return value of timeToWait() is zero. It works that way because we need to avoid spin lock here.

The easiest way to run the tasks from the queue is:
```
  auto tasks = ScheduledTaskQueue{};
  while (!tasks.isEmpty()) {
    auto toWaitInSeconds = tasks.timeToWait();
    if (toWaitInSeconds > 0) {
      std::this_thread::sleep_for(
        std::chrono::seconds{toWaitInSeconds}
      );
    }
    tasks.runOne();
  }
```
See the tests for more details: `osquery/dispatcher/tests/scheduled_tasks_queue_tests.cpp`

