@fmanco, I'm imagining a priority queue of queries ordered by next run time. Then the scheduler loop looks something like (pseudocode):

```
while (!should_stop) {
  current_time = get_current_time();
  query = queue.peek();
  if (query.next_run_time <= current_time) {
    queue.dequeue();
    query.run();
    while (query.next_run_time <= current_time) {
      // If some previous query was really slow, this might skip multiple intervals. That's OK;
      // it's like dropping a frame in an animation
      query.next_run_time += query.interval;
    }
    queue.enqueue(query);
    current_time = get_current_time();  // Refresh current time so we don't oversleep
  }
  next_query = queue.peek();
  sleep(next_query.next_run_time - current_time);
}
```

@theopolis something like the above wouldn't need queries to opt-in; is there a concern with doing it this way?
